WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:14.370
When scientists began observing microbes,
many of the tools that we rely on today, of course,

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.000
had not yet been invented.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.560
Now, microscopes existed, but they were generally
simpler tools.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.170
And they also, of course, lacked cameras that
could take a snapshot of the organisms and

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.170
objects being so closely watched.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:36.120
Still, the scientists were exploring the uncharted
territory of the microcosmos, and words alone

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.840
would not suffice to describe the strange
beings they uncovered.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:47.540
So early microscopic research was not just
a scientific endeavor, it was an artistic

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:54.020
one, relying on illustrations of diatoms and
rotifers and other creatures that could then

00:00:54.020 --> 00:01:00.460
be disseminated to those who were curious
to see these new wonders.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:06.740
One of the most impressive collections of
microbial illustrations was drawn by Christian

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:14.030
Gottfried Ehrenberg, a German naturalist in
the 19th century who amassed thousands and

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:20.979
thousands of geological and biological samples,
and who in turn produced thousands of sketches

00:01:20.979 --> 00:01:24.340
based on the observations he made under the
microscope.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:30.369
Today, we will be taking a bit of a turn from
our normal content, shifting our focus from

00:01:30.369 --> 00:01:38.440
microbes to a scientist whose extensive documentation
of them influenced the fields of microbiology

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.420
and micropaleontology.

00:01:41.429 --> 00:01:47.260
We're going to be talking about Ehrenberg's
life while showing you his drawings, which

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:53.030
come from the collection of his works maintained
at the Museum of Natural History in Berlin.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:02:00.170
And we will also share our own videos of those
same species so you can see how the details

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:06.850
he sketched out by hand stack up against what
our cameras can record from our microscopes

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:08.340
today.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:14.470
So let's start with the early days of Christian
Gottfried Ehrenberg's scientific career.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:21.640
Born in 1795, Ehrenberg earned his doctorate
in 1818 from the University of Berlin.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:29.680
In his thesis, he described 250 species of
fungi from around the city, but most notable

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.080
was his observation that fungi are borne from spores

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.280
and not--as many theorized at the time--spontaneously generated.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:46.480
Two years later, Ehrenberg joined an archaeological
expedition to Egypt that would end up somewhat

00:02:46.489 --> 00:02:47.489
disastrous.

00:02:47.489 --> 00:02:53.790
At one point, the funders claimed to have
"lost" the money meant for the travelers.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:58.400
Then, one of Ehrenberg's compatriots almost
died from a snake bite.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.730
And then later actually died of Typhus.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:07.610
Despite the physical and emotional trials,
Ehrenberg was able to send tens of thousands

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:11.560
of animal, plant, and rock samples to Germany.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:17.659
Except that when he returned home five years
later, many of those samples had been damaged

00:03:17.659 --> 00:03:20.870
and another bunch had been sold.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.000
This was a discouraging outcome.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.250
But when Ehrenberg was offered the chance
to join the esteemed naturalist Alexander

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:34.890
Humboldt on an expedition to Russia and Siberia
in 1829, he seized the opportunity.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:40.540
Fortunately, this expedition turned out to
be much less calamitous.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:46.000
Both of these expeditions provided Ehrenberg
with a wealth of samples to draw from in his

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.700
study of the natural world.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:54.300
And more importantly, they would shape his
fascinations with microbes, which he called

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.209
"Infusoria."

00:03:58.209 --> 00:04:07.099
Ehrenberg was loyal to his microscope, relying
on the same one for decades to study the "Infusoria"

00:04:07.099 --> 00:04:09.319
sampled from various waters.

00:04:09.319 --> 00:04:14.469
He uncovered some of the ways these little
known creatures were connected with the larger

00:04:14.469 --> 00:04:21.150
world they lived in, like the planktonic microorganisms
he realized were the source of phosphorescence

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:27.540
in the Red Sea, and the diatoms whose silica
casings became part of the Earth.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:36.440
Now many of the samples he observed under
the microscope were embedded into mica and

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.460
covered in Canada balsam.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:48.600
Magnifying his various specimens 300x, Ehrenberg
drew out what he saw with a fine grey pencil,sometimes

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.240
adding pigment using watercolor.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:58.960
But no man can be correct all the time, and
Ehrenberg’s refusal to update his microscope

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:03.120
may have led to one of his most notable mistakes.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:09.780
He thought that all of the organisms he observed
must be animals, even the unicellular ones.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:16.420
In some cases, what he claimed were eggs and
sexual organs were later revealed to be nuclei.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:24.140
Still, mistakes aside, his work further solidified
the study of Infusoria, and his collections

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:30.140
and remarks on microbial fossils paved the
way for micropaleontology.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:35.330
While Ehrenberg's later expeditions were not
quite as distant as his first two, this was

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:40.690
an age where naturalists were venturing all
over the globe to gather samples.

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:46.170
And Ehrenberg happened to be corresponding
with quite a few of those naturalists.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:53.070
They would send him bits of living things,
rock, soil, and more from just about everywhere.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:56.110
It's like a postcard for your naturalist pen
pal.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:06:02.770
Charles Darwin even sent him dust that had
blown on to his ship near the Cape Verde islands.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:08.980
In 1854, Ehrenberg published one of his most
famous works: Mikrogeologie.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:12.670
I don’t speak German so I could just say
Microgeology.

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:19.560
It’s a 2-volume text that compiled observations
on North American and European sediments and

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.490
the microbes they housed.

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:29.360
The book would cost you $72, which was not
cheap in 1854, but it might be worth it for

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:34.000
4,000 of these illustrations.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:45.220
Ehrenberg died in 1867, after which his collection
was eventually moved to the Museum of Natural

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:46.520
History in Berlin.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:53.750
Part of the collection's value lies in just
how vast it is, both in the types of samples

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:58.130
it contains and the geographical range they
come from.

00:06:58.130 --> 00:07:04.440
In total, there are more than 40,000 microscope
sample preparations, thousands of specimens

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:07.940
and illustrations, and hundreds of letters.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:14.230
His daughter Clara helped with both the drawings
and compiling an index to link all these samples

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:20.920
together, which has proven essential in making
sense of his immense work.

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:26.650
But the knowledge contained in this collection
would become hard to reach for some time.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:31.970
For decades, a mix of war, politics, and finances
kept it inaccessible.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:36.350
It was even at one point believed to be lost.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:44.440
Fortunately, that was not true, and in the
late 1960s, curators began to restore it.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:50.710
Progress was slow and briefly threatened when
in 1982, a fire broke out in the museum's

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:53.900
roof, close to where the collection was stored.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:59.400
The museum curators responded quickly to save
it, and ultimately these historical documents

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.150
survived with only a little water damage.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:07.390
And through all of these dire situations,
the curators persevered in their intention

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:09.520
to make the collection accessible.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:17.900
In 1998, they released a CD-ROM containing
scanned images of the illustrations, letters,

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:19.430
and indexes.

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:23.840
And now, if you want to explore the images
at your leisure, the museum has made them

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.640
available to download on their website.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.150
And why wouldn’t you?

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:30.820
So there’s a link in the description.

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:35.970
The more recent availability of the collection
has inspired more scientific work.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:42.360
A group of Brazilian scientists studied Ehrenberg's
records on diatom samples taken from their

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:48.870
country, essentially repatriating data and
allowing for a study of biodiversity across

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:50.300
time.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:55.860
Another group of researchers extracted and
examined bacteria from some of Darwin's dust

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:59.770
samples, confirming just how resilient bacteria
can be.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:07.310
Technology, of course, has given us new ways
to document and share the microbial world.

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:12.650
One of the coolest parts of our job as we
assemble each episode of Journey to the Microcosmos

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:19.350
is that we get to spend so much time watching
microbes and appreciating their beauty, a

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:23.540
pursuit that Ehrenberg could surely relate
to.

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:29.540
But the fact that we can record what we see
on video is built on advances in microscopes

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:30.740
and cameras.

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:35.300
Moreover, when we want to share what we see
with the world, we don't have to send you

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:40.040
images via mail or compile them over two decades.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:45.569
We just hit a button on YouTube, and the whole
world can watch if it chooses.

00:09:45.569 --> 00:09:50.389
That same technological progress has made
Ehrenberg's collection more accessible.

00:09:50.389 --> 00:09:57.430
In an age of big data, his illustrations and
his documents are a physical trove of information

00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:00.880
whose promise we're still understanding.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:07.300
It's a common mistake to think of science
and art as separate endeavors, but they're

00:10:07.301 --> 00:10:13.560
linked by creativity and by the human desire
to make sense of the world around us.

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:20.990
Time and invention have only given us more
ways to engage science and art together, and

00:10:20.990 --> 00:10:26.990
in the same way that we've found beauty and
meaning in the stars above us, there are also

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:31.280
answers in the infusoria that surrounds.

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:40.870
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:45.680
Thank you to all of the curators who protected
Ehrenberg’s work, and also, hey, thanks

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:46.910
to Ehrenberg himself.

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:51.139
I know he’s not around to hear it, but his
work is appreciated.

00:10:51.139 --> 00:10:56.339
And of course thank you to all of the people
whose names are on the screen right now.

00:10:56.339 --> 00:10:59.620
If you like this show, they’re the reason
it can exist.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:01.749
They are our supporters on Patreon.

00:11:01.749 --> 00:11:06.420
If you would like to join them, check out
patreon.com/journeytomicro.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.200
There’s a link in the description.

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:12.160
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes James, you can check out Jam and

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:13.399
Germs on Instagram.

00:11:13.399 --> 00:11:19.120
And if you want to see more from us every
week, just go to youtube.com/microcosmos to

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:20.100
see what we’ve been up to.

